          "entradaId": "freeman-222",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Baalim is the plural of Baal. Gesenius defines it “images of Baal.” Against this, however, it has been said that the verbs which are associated in the Bible with the word Baalim are not verbs which are used in connection with images, such as “set up,” “cast down,” “adorn,” or “br…"
      },
      "c-b1734831df118097": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-223",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The early Oriental lock consisted merely of a wooden slide drawn into its place by a string, and fastened there by teeth or catches. The lock commonly used in Egypt and Palestine is a long hollow piece of wood fixed in the door and sliding back and forth. A hole is made for it in…"
      },
      "c-d1197c81a9f7a80a": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-224",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The key was usually of wood, though some have been found in Egypt of iron and bronze. The ordinary wooden key is from six inches to two feet in length, often having a handle of brass or silver, ornamented with filagree work. At the end there are wire pins, which are designed to l…"
      }
    },
